---
description: |
  Detects credential stuffing against login endpoints in OCSF HTTP Activity.

  The operator selects `POST` requests to `paths`, aggregates them per source
  network in tumbling event-time windows, and reports a source when it produces
  at least `min_failures` failed logins that make up at least
  `min_failure_share` of its decided logins. A decided login is a response
  whose status code is in `failure_codes`, which count as failure, or is `200`,
  `204`, `302`, or `303` and not in `failure_codes`, which count as success.
  Other responses, such as `429` or `5xx`, count as attempts but neither as
  failure nor as success. Successful logins from a reported source raise the
  severity from medium to high, because they indicate credentials that worked.

  Sources aggregate to IPv4 addresses and IPv6 `/64` networks, so an attacker
  who rotates addresses within one IPv6 subscriber prefix stays one source.

  Known false negatives:

  - Applications that answer failed logins with `200` or a redirect. Add that
    code to `failure_codes` when only failed logins receive it, so that it no
    longer counts as success, or rewrite `http_response.code` upstream when
    only the response body or the redirect target tells the outcome.
  - Distributed attacks that rotate through residential proxies or IPv4
    addresses in different networks, and low-and-slow attacks below
    `min_failures` per window. HTTP logs rarely carry the username, so the
    operator can't key on the targeted accounts either. Use
    `tenzir::detect::authentication::credential_stuffing` on identity provider
    logs for those.
  - Bursts that straddle a window boundary and stay below the thresholds on
    both sides. A burst of at least twice `min_failures` failures within
    `window_size` always reaches the threshold in one window.

  Known false positives:

  - A misconfigured client or service account that retries a stale password,
    because the operator can't tell many accounts from one.
  - Security scanners, load tests, and uptime checks against the login path.
  - Shared egress such as NAT, CGNAT, or a corporate proxy, when its failures
    dominate. The failure share keeps busy shared addresses with mostly
    successful logins quiet.
  - Several records for the same request, such as WAF events next to request
    logs, which inflate the counts. Feed one record per request.

  Without a username, the operator can't distinguish credential stuffing from
  brute force against one account, so findings cover both.
args:
  named:
    - name: window_size
      type: duration
      default: 5min
      description: Event-time window over which to count login attempts.
    - name: tolerance
      type: duration
      default: 1min
      description: Maximum expected lateness for out-of-order events.
    - name: idle_timeout
      type: duration
      default: 5min
      description: Inactivity after which the current window closes.
    - name: paths
      default: ["/login"]
      description: List of URL paths that accept credentials, matched exactly.
    - name: failure_codes
      default: [401, 403]
      description: List of HTTP status codes that indicate a failed login.
    - name: min_failures
      type: int
      default: 50
      description: Minimum number of failed logins from one source network.
    - name: min_failure_share
      type: float
      default: 0.9
      description: Minimum share of failed logins among decided logins.
---

let $analytic = "tenzir::detect::http::credential_stuffing"

where class_uid == 4002 and
      http_request.url.path in $paths and
      http_request.http_method == "POST" and
      src_endpoint.ip != null
// The summarize key must be a field, so compute the source network once here.
source = subnet(src_endpoint.ip, 64 if src_endpoint.ip.is_v6() else 32)
window size=$window_size, on=time, tolerance=$tolerance,
       idle_timeout=$idle_timeout {
  summarize source,
            attempts=count(),
            failures=count_if(http_response.code, x => x in $failure_codes),
            successes=count_if(http_response.code,
                               x => x in [200, 204, 302, 303] and
                                    x not in $failure_codes),
            ips=distinct(src_endpoint.ip),
            user_agents=distinct(http_request.user_agent?),
            first=min(time),
            last=max(time)
  start = $window.start
}
where failures >= $min_failures and
      failures >= (failures + successes) * $min_failure_share
origin = f"{ips[0] if ips.length() == 1 else source}"
this = {
  time: now(),
  metadata: {
    product: {name: "Tenzir", vendor_name: "Tenzir"},
    uid: uuid(),
    version: "1.9.0",
  },
  category_uid: 2,
  class_uid: 2004,
  activity_id: 1,
  type_uid: 200401,
  severity_id: 4 if successes > 0 else 3,
  status_id: 1,
  is_alert: true,
  start_time: first,
  end_time: last,
  finding_info: {
    uid: f"{$analytic}:{source}:{start}",
    title: f"Credential stuffing from {origin}",
    desc: f"{failures} of {attempts} logins from {origin} failed and {successes} succeeded; source addresses: {ips.length()}, user agents: {user_agents.length()}",
    analytic: {
      name: "Credential stuffing",
      uid: $analytic,
      type_id: 1,
      version: "1.0",
    },
    attacks: [
      {
        tactic: {uid: "TA0006", name: "Credential Access"},
        technique: {uid: "T1110", name: "Brute Force"},
        sub_technique: {uid: "T1110.004", name: "Credential Stuffing"},
      },
    ],
  },
  evidences: ips.sort().slice(end=10).map(ip => {
    src_endpoint: {ip: ip},
    // Attribute a user agent only when the source used exactly one.
    http_request: {user_agent: user_agents[0]} if user_agents.length() == 1,
  }),
}
